  // previous: 0.39.240 —   // [0.39.240: James, after 0.39.239's Library Responses tab: "also needs to be clearglass library. like added to the 3 lines menu" -- and, correcting my first reading (the page menus in ui/home and ui/tv-shell): "no. clearglass/renderer/index.html". Clear Glass's window is renderer/browser.html + browser.js (there is no renderer/index.html). Its ☰ menu (#bookmark-mgr-btn) had Downloads/Site Settings/History/Background Tabs; the Library window was tray-only. Added a fixed '📚 Library' row after History that opens the SAME window the tray does (agentId nexus-library, /ui/library/) via cg.window.open; failures toasted. PROVEN by clicking it in headless Chromium on the real browser.html with window.ClearGlass stood in from the real preload's shape: one cg.window.open call with exactly the tray's agentId and URL, menu closed, no page errors (tests/probe/clearglass-menu-library.py, derives everything from source at run time). clear-glass-library-ui 24 -> 28. clear-glass 3.12.1.]   // [0.39.239: James: "the download manager in clearglass needs a ui so i can actually see this visually" / "the download manager should be routing the response to the agent or compartment id." MAPPED: guardian has written every completed reply to Clear Glass's downloads index since 2026-09-20 (code-artifact.js recordAgentResponse -> artifact-chat-index.js, COS compartment clearglass-downloads-index) with agentId inside raw, and nothing read it back; the Library window (tray -> Library) was the existing manager UI but listed only browser downloads. BUILT: agent_id/job_id first-class in the index (older files read from raw), readItem, ONE root resolver defaultRoot() used by all four callers (they each resolved it before); Clear Glass GET /cli/downloads/responses[/:id]; a Library Responses tab (ui/library/areas/responses.js + .css) listing replies by agent with text and code blocks. FIXED ON THE WAY: download-capture attached one will-download listener per provider WINDOW on one shared session (every download announced once per open window) -> idempotent per session; downloads now carry the tab's agentId; the ledger recorded the provider name as the agent; the bridge's command index ignored the test sandbox (a test starting it rewrote the real data/clear-glass/command-index.json). Tests: NEW test-downloads-responses 15/15 (real guardian writer, real bridge over HTTP, real capture; each fix mutation-checked); tab driven in headless Chromium, no console errors. clear-glass 3.12.0 (registry 97 -> 99, contract regenerated), guardian 3.9.2.]   // [0.39.238: James, with the 2026-09-25 log (repo job 44d68e3d: own tab, delivered, acked, submitted, then silence) and the reply visible in ChatGPT: "this should be working." The 0.39.224 stage evidence answered it -- no reply-started, so the watch never saw reply text. Every userscript's checkStable() rescheduled itself forever when findResponseEl() found nothing (the NO_REPLY_MS deadline sat below that branch). Fixed in all six: a 'no-reply-element' progress stage after 15s and a loud GUARDIAN_ERROR at the deadline naming selector drift. ChatGPT's findResponseEl() now returns the streaming element or the LAST assistant message (querySelector + :last-child returned the first, or nothing). Userscripts bumped with header/VERSION aligned. test-guardian-job-correlation 102 -> 120; the 0.39.237 scripts fail all 16 new checks and hang. guardian 3.9.0 -> 3.9.1. Also recorded: the boot vitals regressions (dispatcher-stale-socket never exits, copilot-provider-toggle T-018, spec-engine-yaml-import) are identical on 0.39.236 -- pre-existing, not from 0.39.237.]   // [0.39.237: James, against his 2026-09-25 boot log (a repo-agent job for "ERAVOS v3-17 catalog"): "it shouldn't be opening two instances. this needs to work end to end." MAPPED, four causes, all fixed: (1) guardian/lib/dispatcher.js sent a job with an agentId to the SHARED tab when the repo's own tab had not registered, and in the same call opened the repo tab for next time -- the job ran in the shared window, the new one sat idle, two ChatGPT instances stood from then on. Now the job goes to its own tab or waits for it (queued once, reason named, flushQueuedJobs delivers on connect); the shared tab is a logged, emitted (guardian.job.agent_tab_fallback), .job-recorded fallback only when Clear Glass cannot open the tab or it has not connected within GUARDIAN_AGENT_TAB_WAIT_MS (60s). (2) cold start asked Clear Glass for the shared window, which then caused (1); now it opens the repo tab. (3) clear-glass/src/providers/host.js restarted a crashed or unexpectedly closed agent tab as the SHARED window; now as the same agent tab. (4) closeAgentTab (idle sweep, LRU cap) did not mark the close intentional, so 5s later the close handler started the shared window -- every idle repo tab left a ChatGPT behind. Also: the userscripts' IndexedDB replay-on-connect removed from all six (it re-sent other windows' records from an origin-wide store; guardian never matched them, records carry uuid not jobId -- the 'GUARDIAN_REPLAY ack ... undefined' line), and guardian's GUARDIAN_REPLAY case no longer marks a job complete with no response. Tests: dispatcher-agent-tab-trigger 10/10 (was 5; AT-002/AT-005 pinned the shared-tab delivery and were reversed), NEW test-provider-host-one-tab 7/7 against the real ProviderHost class (only electron faked), NEW test-guardian-replay-retired 3/3 (HEAD's handler fails RR-01); every fix mutation-checked. Regression: provider-host-respawn 4 (source pin moved to the fixed line), ncp-handler-sr3 7, job-correlation 102, chat-sync-agent-routing 8, ncp-hostile-payload 5, job-persistence 15, cg-job-intake 12, clear-glass-pressure 12, agent-mesh coverage 7, version-sync 30, record-discipline 9, spawn-provider-tab exit 0. guardian 3.8.0 -> 3.9.0, clear-glass 3.11.2 -> 3.11.3 (eight sync points, including registry-components.js's header comment). CORRECTION to 0.39.228's note below: the home-split probe compares 40 computed properties, not 45. Not done: the ping gate pings the active client, not the repo tab.]   // [0.39.236: James, with the Idearium Repos tab showing only inject-test-*, prov-*, phase-sync-test-* and phase-sync-standalone-*: "the tests need to stop in idearium. they keep generating." Third cleanup of this class (0.39.121 bl7-*, 2026-09-06's 63 specs, 0.39.188's clear-idearium), so the cause was fixed, not the symptom. CAUSE: isolation was opt-in per test (IDEARIUM_DATA_DIR, JAA_DATA_DIR), most tests never opted in, COMPARTMENT OS had no override at all, and idearium/repo/repo-node.js + idearium/lib/chunk-nodes.js ignored IDEARIUM_DATA_DIR entirely. Leaked specs were then adopted at boot by _reconcileSpecRepos into real compartments, built by the build queue, and dispatched to ChatGPT as repo-agent jobs (James's 2026-09-25 log: job 0631e27a for inject-test-1790345265876). One unpatched suite run measured here: 422 idearium node files, 29 spec files, 80 project files, 141 COS files left behind. FIX, the default flipped: NEW lib/test-sandbox.js -- a test process (entry under tests/ or a test/ dir, *.test.js, or marked by a runner) gets ONE temp root and every store's existing override pointed into it (IDEARIUM_DATA_DIR, JAA_DATA_DIR, COS_DATA_ROOT (new, cos/foundation/constants.js), NEXUS_INJECT_DIR, NEXUS_DATA_ROOT); set on process.env so spawned NEXUS processes inherit it; removed on exit. Called by the resolvers themselves (NEW idearium/lib/data-dir.cjs -- the one idearium data-path resolver, 8 sites routed through it + the event ledger; cortex/memory/jaa-db.js; COS constants; lib/repo-inject.js; lib/ledger-writer.js; intelligence/alk), so a test cannot forget. Runners give each suite its own root: tests/modules/run-all.js, autopilot's post-boot vitals check and container test run, scripts/precommit-check.js's require() probe (which starts real servers from the live tree). 27 tests that start NEXUS processes call ensure() first; spec-list-merge.test.mjs moved off the real :4800 (it posted to James's live idearium when running). seedFromReal() lets the two tests whose point is reading the live store (tablet graph, ALK lattice) read a COPY. Also: autopilot's vitals check was killed at 30s while running 9 suites of up to 30s each -- the 'VITALS CHECK FAILED (no summary line captured)' on every boot; now 180s. CLEANUP: cli/clear-idearium.js 0.1.0 -> 0.2.0 (extended, not a second tool): repo-scoped tables, inject nodes, idearium-repo-*/idearium-idea-* COS compartments, and guardian .job files carrying a cleared repo's project-agent prompt; --apply refuses while idearium :4800 or guardian :7820 answers; uses the same resolvers as the stores. Shipped idearium/data cleared of its test output (25 repository nodes, 442 chunk nodes, 10 projects, 8 specs -- all traced to their tests). TESTS: NEW test-test-sandbox 28/28 (a careless test in a real child process creates spec+repo+compartment; nothing new or grown in real idearium/data, data/cortex/memory, data/idearium; no autopilot entry point taken for a test; static guards), NEW test-clear-idearium 14/14 (real stores in a sandbox; unrelated job and compartment survive; live refusal). Mutation-checked: sandbox disabled -> 9 fail; repo-node reverted -> 1; old ledger path -> 1; job filter removed -> 2; refusal removed -> 1. Full suite, parallel, vs the unmodified tree: identical except test-alk-lattice-live and test-tablet-graph-api, which passed before only because earlier tests had filled the real store; against a populated store they match baseline exactly and leave it byte-identical. After the patched full run: 0 new/changed files in idearium/data, 0 COS compartments. NOT COVERED, stated: other systems' own hardcoded stores still take test writes (guardian data/guardian/ledger + code-artifacts, versionium data/versionium, intelligence/data/nodes, copilot person-model ledgers, component_ledger files) -- same fix shape, not done unasked. idearium 4.2.0 -> 4.2.1.] [0.39.235: James, with a screenshot of a ChatGPT project chat + a full boot log: "jobs aren't resolving to idearium. can reuse, job files. what about using raid?" First hypothesis (mine) was wrong and James corrected it directly: "idearium is for coding. it needs to give the agents response in the cli." MAPPED, not guessed, before the correction and after: the log's job (df5fa7cc, hat:the_builder, "project agent for ERAVOS v3-17 catalog") is a lib/repo-agent.js dispatch (agentId `repo-<uuid>`), not an idearium spec-chunk job — idearium/lib/guardian-stream.cjs only ever resolves chunks matched by `chunk.jobId` on a chunk at status:'building' ("most guardian jobs are not idearium chunks at all" is its own documented, correct behavior, not a bug). RAID (cortex/core/raid) is a provider-ROUTING engine (LAW_I/II/III, health/fitness ranking) with a real, already-wired feedback loop (guardian/lib/raid-feedback.js → cortex /api/raid/feedback → recordOutcome, confirmed firing for every job including this one, DESPITE the diagnostic flagging guardian.job.dispatch.complete→cortex.raid.feedback as a dangling hook — that's a false positive from the static hook-name scanner, the real wiring exists under different literal names) — it updates RAID's own per-(agent,cluster) success-rate weights, nothing about idearium or the CLI. Neither RAID nor idearium's resolver was ever the right lever for this. THE REAL GAP, found after James's correction: cli/nexus-repl.js's startGuardianStream() — the background SSE mirror that's "what makes the terminal mirror the UI in real-time" — has always printed guardian.job.* EVENTS only (the label, an 8-char jobId prefix, the provider) and NEVER the agent's actual reply text, for every job dispatched from anywhere, not specific to repo-agent. A job resolves async, off-CLI, and the terminal James is driving from never shows what came back — exactly "give me the agent's response in the cli." Job files reused as asked, not rebuilt: guardian's own GET /response/:jobId (registry-components.js's job.response route, backed by the same durable .job-file record idearium/lib/guardian-stream.cjs already reads) is the real, existing, single source for a job's resolved text — one lookup added on guardian.job.complete specifically, fire-and-forget async IIFE (the outer SSE data handler is synchronous per chunk and must not block on it), printed truncated at 800 chars with a pointer to the full endpoint for longer replies, silent on any fetch failure (guardian restarting, an unrelated jobId) rather than breaking the SSE mirror. Verified live: real, unmodified cli/nexus-repl.js run as a real child process against a minimal stand-in server exposing guardian's exact two real route shapes (/events SSE, /response/:jobId JSON — payload shapes checked against guardian/server.js itself, not invented) emitting the real job.queued/dispatched/complete sequence for df5fa7cc — confirmed the terminal printed the exact reply text from James's own screenshot ("Understood. I'll treat ERAVOS v3-17 catalog as the only project scope...") under the `[job] complete` line. Separately noted, NOT fixed here (out of scope, a different pre-existing bug found while reading this file): cli/nexus-repl.js's runLocalCommand fallback (~line 1086) references cmdJobs/cmdGaps/cmdLedger/cmdEvents/cmdIdeas/cmdDispatch/cmdSNR — none of these functions are defined anywhere in the file; typing `jobs`/`gaps`/etc. with guardian unreachable would throw ReferenceError. Flagged for James, not silently carried forward.] [0.39.234: James, screenshot of Clear Glass's own real "Customize Toolbar" dialog: "WHERE IS IT? anything outside of this, is wrong. index.html." Mapped before touching anything, reading browser.js's actual dialog renderer (not assumed from the commands file alone): the dialog has TWO real surfaces — a draggable Toolbar/⋯-Menu zone built from `TOOLBAR_COMMANDS.filter(c => c.id && c.pinnable)` (line ~1483), and a click-to-run "Actions" list at the bottom built from `filter(c => !c.id ...)` for palette-only commands with no backing button. src/toolbar/commands.js's `settings` entry had no `id` — `action: 'settings'` only — so per this file's own documented rule ("Palette-only actions (no id) are never pinnable — there's no button to pin") it was structurally excluded from BOTH the pinnable drag zones AND the live ⋯ menu's auto-populated "Tools" section (same `c.id && c.pinnable` filter, browser.js line ~1440) — it only ever showed in the small, scrollable, easy-to-miss "Actions" list, mislabeled "API Settings…" and grouped under "Window" next to New Agent Window / Mesh Queue. Real fix, not a relabel: gave it a real id (`btn-settings`), `pinnable: true`, a real backing `<button id="btn-settings">` in browser.html's toolbar-overflow-slot (same convention as btn-mesh/btn-diag), and a click handler calling the same `cg.window.openSettings()` every other real Settings entry point already uses (tray menu, the nexus:open-settings webview bridge added at 0.39.233). SECOND, real gap found while wiring this in, not assumed away: the ⋯ menu already had a hardcoded `"☰ Settings"` row (data-action="settings") that actually opened the unrelated Nexus Options panel (openOptionsPanel()) — a pre-existing naming collision that would have put two different things both labeled "Settings" in the same menu once btn-settings's own Tools row appeared beside it. Deleted that hardcoded row and its handler rather than relabeling around it: Nexus Options keeps its own real name and its own existing toolbar-button + Tools-row access (btn-options, unchanged), and there is now exactly one thing in the entire UI named "Settings," doing exactly one thing, reachable both as a draggable chip in this exact Customize Toolbar dialog and as an auto-listed Tools row in the live ⋯ menu once unpinned — the Firefox model James pointed at. Also removed the now-dead `case 'settings':` in activateToolbarCommand's switch (unreachable once cmd.id is set — `if (cmd.id) { ...click(); return; }` always fires first) and a stale comment referencing it. Verified live, not asserted from source: booted the real orchestrator + real Electron under Xvfb, connected via CDP/Playwright, drove the actual running app — opened the real ⋯ menu (confirmed "⚙ Settings" listed once, correctly, under TOOLS, no duplicate), opened the real Customize Toolbar dialog via a genuine click on its own menu item (confirmed the "⚙ Settings" chip present in the ⋯ MENU drop zone, screenshotted), dragged that same real chip into the Toolbar zone and clicked Done (confirmed the real #btn-settings DOM node relocated into #toolbar-pinned-tools with display:flex — the same real relocate-the-actual-node mechanism every other pinnable tool uses, not a mock), then clicked the real button and confirmed a genuine new BrowserWindow opened at renderer/settings.html — not a function call bypassing the click path. store.js's defaultPinnedIds() re-derived cleanly from the changed registry with zero other edits needed (checked directly: `node -e` against the real file). No clear-glass version bump — its six sync points are unrelated to this file (a toolbar-command registry entry and its two consuming renderer functions, not a new IPC route or main-process surface).] [0.39.233: James: "the three lines menu. its not here. at all. getting fuming angry at this. fix it. the index.html." Real gap, mapped before touching anything: ui/tv-shell/index.html (the actual default homepage since 0.39.229's fix) and ui/home/index.html both have a real menu overlay (#sys-bar, INSPECT + REWIND buttons) but neither one had any way to reach Settings — every settings.html section built this session (Downloads, Plugins, and everything before) was reachable only through Clear Glass's OWN browser.html chrome (the ☰ button → options panel → "API Settings…" link), completely invisible from the page James considers the real app. Root cause of why it looked like nothing existed: the two pages he actually opens have zero settings entry point of any kind, Firefox-style or otherwise. FIX: a real "⚙ SETTINGS" button added to both pages' #sys-bar, next to INSPECT/REWIND. Both pages run inside Clear Glass's own <webview> (contextIsolation, no Node, no window.ClearGlass — that global exists only on the outer browser.html document), so the button can't call cg.window.openSettings() directly; it sends a new 'nexus:open-settings' message through the existing window.__cg bridge (webview-bridge.js's contextBridge, previously allowlisted for dom:pick-result/dom:event/nexus:cookie-request only — added this fourth channel, same pattern, not a new mechanism), which browser.js's existing wv ipc-message listener now catches and turns into the real cg.window.openSettings() call — same function the tray menu item already uses, so this isn't a second, parallel settings surface. New ui/home/areas/settings-btn.js + settings-btn.css (matches that page's existing one-JS-plus-one-CSS-per-area convention from the 0.39.227/228 split); tv-shell's version is inline (matches that file's own existing all-inline-script convention) plus a new #settings-btn rule in tv-shell.css alongside the existing #inspect-btn/#rewind-btn rules it was styled to match. Verified live, not asserted from source: booted the real orchestrator (serving the real edited tv-shell/index.html at :9000) and the real Electron app under Xvfb, connected via CDP, drove the webview to the real default URL, called wv.executeJavaScript to confirm #settings-btn exists in the live guest page, then executed a REAL click on it inside that guest page (not a direct function call bypassing the click path) — the real settings.html window opened, screenshotted, showing the full Identity/Agents/Browser/System rail including this session's own Downloads and Plugins sections. No registry/version-sync entries needed — no new clear-glass IPC route (nexus:open-settings is a webview-to-host bridge message, not an ipcMain handler, so it never touches registry-components.js or interaction-contract.json) and no clear-glass version bump (its own six sync points are unrelated to a page it doesn't own — ui/ is orchestrator's, not clear-glass's).] [0.39.232: James: "give me the project back. nexus. no fragments. no monkey patches. give me the updated project, bumped a version" — rejected the prior 231 delivery, which shipped only the changed-files patch bundle (an incremental delta) rather than the full, real project. No source changes this bump — packaging-only, real per §17.9's own honesty requirement (a version bump must name an actual improvement; this one's improvement is delivery completeness, stated as such, not disguised as a feature bump). Full working tree (minus .git, node_modules, and every real `data`/`memory_store` state directory — CLAUDE.md's own delivery discipline: "NEVER commit data/**. That is state, not code.") packaged and shipped as one zip, Nexus_v0_39_232.zip, no changed-files fragment this time.] [0.39.231: James: "Yes build all the ui" (confirming the Firefox-reference settings gap pass, after "Okay but where is the rebuild UIs? Agent mesh yi? Setting?" surfaced that accounts/mesh/suite already existed but the person had never seen them, root-caused to the 0.39.229 defaultStartUrl bug). Mapped every Firefox category James's reference screenshot named to real backend capability before building anything (non-negotiable: map first, no theater) — grepped clear-glass/src and clear-glass/renderer directly for each. Two categories had real capability sitting unexposed: Downloads (cg.downloads.list/clearItem/openFolder have been real IPC handlers since 2026-08-24, never called by any section except privacy.js's bulk clearCompleted) and "Extensions and themes" (src/plugins/host.js's list()/disable() existed since 2026-08-24 with zero IPC exposure — added plugins:list/plugins:disable to clear-glass/src/ipc/bridge.js as thin wrappers over those same real methods, plus preload/index.js, plus list() now also returns displayName/description which were already on every manifest and simply never returned). Built clear-glass/renderer/settings/sections/downloads.js (real per-item download list, save-location field) and sections/plugins.js (lists all 6 real installed plugins with real manifest text, real Disable control) — the latter named "Plugins," not "Extensions and themes," because themes have zero backend anywhere in this codebase, confirmed by direct grep, not assumed; a theme toggle would have been exactly the fabrication the non-negotiables forbid. Added a small real "Page zoom" control to General, wired to the zoom plugin's already-real toolbar-command signatures via plugins:invoke (fire-and-forget by that handler's own documented design, so the confirmation toast says the command was sent, not a zoom factor this call can't see). Registered both new sections in settings.html before boot.js (which must stay last). FOUND, NOT BUILT, surfaced honestly rather than stubbed: no real backend anywhere for search-engine selection, appearance/theme (dark/light mode), or accessibility settings; "language" only exists as the fingerprint-spoofing profile's field, already covered by sections/fingerprint.js, not a general preference; backgroundTabDefaults.openInBackground (pre-existing, already a toggle in General) still has zero real consumer anywhere, reconfirmed directly, left untouched. Recorded as clear-glass.spec gap FGAP1. Verified live, not just read from source: real Electron app booted under Xvfb (--no-sandbox, this container runs as root), connected via CDP/Playwright at the real running process's devtools port, opened the real Settings window via window.ClearGlass.window.openSettings(), rendered all three pages with real data (6 real installed plugins with their real manifest descriptions and "active" state; live options.get() for the download folder field), then actually clicked "Disable" on the zoom plugin's row and confirmed its state flipped to "disabled" on screen — a real round trip through the new IPC handlers to the real pluginHost.disable(), not asserted from code reading. clear-glass 3.11.1 -> 3.11.2, all six sync points moved together (package.json, clear-glass.spec meta.version + a new history: entry + a new version_history: entry, this services map entry, main/index.js's CG_VERSION, registry-components.js's V constant + header comment); interaction-contract.json's own version bumped to match but its routes are untouched (no new registry-components.js entries — plugins:list/plugins:disable are new IPC routes on the existing namespace, not new registry-tracked components, and the contract states its routes are derived from the registry alone). tests/modules/version-sync-and-registry.test.js's hardcoded 3.11.1 literals bumped to 3.11.2 to match.] [0.39.230: James, replying to the 0.39.229 fix with a screenshot: "doesn't look any different" — the URL bar in his screenshot already showed http://127.0.0.1:9000/, proving 229's fix DID work (the renderer really did navigate to the real homepage), but the page itself was a black screen with only a faint starfield. Root cause, confirmed live, not guessed: orchestrator.js's root '/' route serves ui/tv-shell/index.html's raw content AT the URL '/', but that file's own asset tags are authored relative to its real on-disk location — <script src="./warp-ui/warp-browser.js">, src="../lib/api.js", href="./spotlight/spotlight.css", href="../themes/nexus-dark.css" — and a browser resolves '.'/'..' against the document's URL, not the file's real path. Served at '/', every one of those requests a bare top-level path (/warp-ui/warp-browser.js, /lib/api.js, etc.) that hit a blanket `if (top !== 'api') return 404` gate earlier in the same router function, always, for every person who has ever loaded this page directly — not a regression from 229, a pre-existing bug 229 simply exposed for the first time by finally pointing anything real at this URL. Verified with a live orchestrator instance (curl before/after) and a real headless-Chromium render (Playwright): before the fix, 8/8 of tv-shell's own script/css requests 404'd and only its inline <script> blocks ran (the dark background + faint canvas dots James's screenshot shows); after, all 8 load 200 and the page renders its real content — system tiles for orchestrator/guardian/cortex/idearium/bridge/architect/loom/emerge/forge-shell/agents/blueprint-builder, each with live route/job/gap counts. FIX: a real, guarded static-file fallback added to orchestrator.js's router, placed correctly this time — a first attempt was written right before the router's OWN final 404 near EOF and verified DEAD on a live server (the earlier `top !== 'api'` gate always fires first and returns before that code could run); moved to just above that gate. For any GET nothing above already matched, tries UI_ROOT/<path> then UI_ROOT/tv-shell/<path>, in that order (covering tv-shell's '../x' and './x' references respectively), serving only a real file that genuinely exists on disk via the same _safePath traversal guard every other static route in this file already uses — a truly bogus path still falls through to the ordinary JSON 404, unchanged (verified). Same bug class already named and fixed once for idearium elsewhere in this file (§TRAILING-SLASH FIX 2026-07-15), but idearium's fix (redirect into a directory URL) doesn't apply here — tv-shell is deliberately served AT '/', by an explicit prior decision already recorded in this file's own comments, not at a nested path it could redirect into. orchestrator 2.2.0 -> 2.2.1 (PATCH: real bug fix, no new registered route or API surface) in both real sync points — lib/version.js services.orchestrator, orchestrator/spec/orchestrator.spec meta.version (orchestrator carries no separate registry-components.js V constant or interaction-contract version tied to its release number — checked directly, not assumed; interaction-contract.json's "1.0.0" is an independent contract-schema version, untouched).]   // [0.39.229: James: "the ui, its not changed at all... just fix it. wasted countless tokens." Mapped first, as asked, before touching anything: ui/home/index.html is confirmed real, confirmed Electron-rendered (clear-glass/src/main/index.js's "NEXUS Home UI" menu item calls openAgentWindow({agentId:'nexus-home', url:'http://127.0.0.1:9000/ui/home/index.html'}) -- a real BrowserWindow, navigated to a URL the orchestrator genuinely serves) -- every UI build shipped this whole engagement, home UI split included, was real and reachable. The actual bug: src/main/index.js's boot path (§18, "Default window") opens the app's one real STARTUP window with nexusOptions.get().defaultStartUrl, and src/options/store.js's DEFAULTS.defaultStartUrl has been the literal string 'about:blank' since before this options store existed. So every session, every build, the app opened to a blank page by default, regardless of what UI work landed -- not because the UI wasn't built, because nothing ever pointed the renderer at it. Confirmed this is the root cause of the standing "nothing in front end" complaint, not a new regression. FIX: DEFAULTS.defaultStartUrl -> 'http://127.0.0.1:9000/', orchestrator's own root '/', which already, explicitly, by a prior decision recorded in orchestrator.js's own comments, serves ui/tv-shell/index.html (3,227 lines, real, 10 scripts) as the homepage -- not a new choice invented here, the existing intended one, just finally wired to the thing that actually opens on launch. SECOND, LESS OBVIOUS BUG: load()'s own `{...DEFAULTS, ...raw}` merge means a real, already-existing nexus-options.json (every real install so far -- the constructor's first-ever boot calls _persist(), writing 'about:blank' to disk explicitly) would silently keep overriding this new default forever, no matter how many future sessions change DEFAULTS -- a fix that only ever helps a fresh install nobody has. Added a real, versioned, one-time migration (_schemaVersion, currently 1): a file below the current version gets defaultStartUrl upgraded ONLY if it is still exactly the untouched old literal 'about:blank' (undefined counts as untouched too); a person's own real choice, INCLUDING deliberately picking about:blank again after this migration has already run once, is never overwritten. Checked, not guessed: grepped every other 'about:blank' reference in clear-glass/src and clear-glass/renderer first -- all of them are the correct, unrelated "empty tab" placeholder for new tabs / webview defaults, not startup behavior; none needed touching. src/main/index.pre-userscripts.js also hardcodes 'about:blank' as an openAgentWindow default parameter but is confirmed dead (zero requires anywhere in the tree) -- left alone, not a live code path. Tests: NEW 5 cases in tests/modules/nexus-options-autoboot.test.js -- fresh install gets the real homepage; an existing about:blank file is migrated exactly once; a real, already-customized start URL survives untouched; about:blank deliberately re-chosen AFTER migration (schema already current) is never re-overwritten; the migrated value round-trips through a second, independent load() (persisted, not re-derived). 15/15 (was 10/10). Regression: clear-glass-accounts 11/12 (1 pre-existing failure, confirmed identical with this fix stashed out -- needs a live guardian server this environment doesn't have, unrelated), clear-glass-autofill 16/16, cg-accounts-portal-settings 38/38, guardian-agent-registry 11/11. clear-glass 3.11.0 -> 3.11.1 (PATCH: real bug fix, no new API surface) in all four sync points -- lib/version.js services['clear-glass'], clear-glass/spec/clear-glass.spec meta.version, clear-glass/src/main/index.js's CG_VERSION, clear-glass/package.json.]   // [0.39.228: NEXUS home UI (ui/home/index.html) -- one JS + one CSS file per area, the rule James set in the 0.39.223 handoff ("should have had the ui aspects separate files, including css file per ui file"). The handoff first aimed this at clear-glass/renderer/browser.js; James corrected it: "The ui is the index.html file." MAPPED FIRST: CSS was already external (home.css, 941 lines, shared); the monolith was the JS (1,950 + 188 inline lines). ui/home/nexus-home.html (July's decomposed shell) points at 11 channel documents of which only 4 exist -- 7 were lost before git history starts (2026-09-17) -- so index.html is the only working home (Clear Glass's menu opens it) and was split IN PLACE, not cut over. index.html 2,943 -> 835 lines, markup only; 20 areas in ui/home/areas/ (JS + CSS each), 6 non-UI modules in ui/home/core/ (config, keys, sse, ui-state, logging, boot); home.css keeps tokens, reset, shared keyframes and components several channels use. Cuts are CONTIGUOUS ranges of the original script, in original order, so the split is provable. 43 lines of home.css deleted (identical to ui/tv-shell/spotlight/spotlight.css, which loads earlier, nothing between targets them); the duplicate .sys-tile transition was NOT deleted -- it overrides the earlier .sys-tile rule. BUG FOUND AND FIXED: with a mode in localStorage, initMode -> selectMode -> _logToData touched _uiLog in its temporal dead zone and threw, halting the rest of the inline script -- pollHealth, connectSSE and every interval never started, and every later _logToData threw. Anyone who had ever picked a mode got a home UI with no health and no live events. core/logging.js now loads first. PROOF: tests/probe/home-split-equivalence.py -- real headless Chromium, both sides served identically, non-local requests aborted, CSS animation time frozen; reference = the pre-split monolith from git with only the logging fix applied; with and without a stored mode, every channel and overlay state, 126 top-level names, 45 computed properties per element: EQUIVALENT. Tests: NEW test-home-ui-files 14/14 (7 mutation checks); home-ui 22/22 (was 19: its 3 CSS checks read index.html after the CSS had moved to home.css -- now read the assembled page via tests/helpers/home-page-source.js); nexus-full-audit 87 -> 90 (same cause). Records: docs/home-ui.spec 1.3.0 -> 1.4.0; loom/maps/ui-map.js registers the home page (49 components, 48 load wires, 0 failures, derived from index.html's own tags). No service version: home-ui is not a service in this file.]   // [0.39.227: James: "Do it. 1 then 2." (1) CLEAR GLASS JOB INTAKE -- the design question 0.39.225/226 left open in guardian.spec (watch vs push, which transport owns a job) DECIDED: push-with-claim. guardian's ladder CLAIMS the job on its .job (transport:'mesh', claimedBy:'clear-glass', a REQUIRED write -- updateJob gained an opt-in {required}) before any send; a failed claim sends nothing and falls to NCP with reason mesh_claim_failed; the dispatcher's NCP fallback now also clears claimedBy, so the .job never names two owners. clear-glass/src/jobs/intake.js is Clear Glass's intake: it accepts a job ONLY after reading it back through guardian's own GET /jobs?id= and finding the claim (no claim / unknown / finished / provider mismatch / guardian unreachable = refused, stage intake_refused, sent:false), writes its OWN <jobId>.intake record before queueing, is idempotent by jobId, and after a Clear Glass restart reports unfinished jobs as clear_glass_restarted with sent:null (never resend). /agent-mesh/send and /agent-mesh/job go through it; new GET /agent-mesh/intake lists records. Not a watch on guardian's jobs folder: that breaks the node-ownership rule (only guardian reads its .job files) and would pick up NCP-owned jobs too -- the duplicate class guardian.spec warned about. (2) UI FILES -- Settings sections split one area per file (14 JS) with one CSS file per JS file; settings.css keeps shared tokens/frame/components only; area CSS scoped to body[data-area] and loaded by core.js when the area is shown. Tests: test-cg-job-intake 12/12 (real guardian store, ladder, mesh-client, both HTTP servers; mutation-checked 2/3/1 fail), test-cg-settings-ui-files 6/6 (leak mutation caught), persistence 15/15 (one source check re-pointed at the new updateJob signature), ladder 18, correlation 102, record-discipline 9, version-sync 30, accounts-portal-settings 38, mesh coverage 7. clear-glass 3.10.0 -> 3.11.0, guardian 3.7.0 -> 3.8.0.]   // [0.39.226: handoff item 3 for guardian: the records catch up with the code, and the rule gets a test. 0.39.223 shipped guardian's account authority in code (lib/cg-account-authority.js, agent-registry accountAuthority, dispatch-ladder awaiting resolveAccountAsync) and updated docs/atlases/guardian-atlas.md, but NOT guardian/spec/guardian.spec -- its own handoff names this as the thing to do first. Done here, together with the two changes I added since: guardian 3.6.2 -> 3.7.0 (MINOR: new capability, nothing removed, every existing route and event unchanged) in all three places that state it -- lib/version.js services.guardian, guardian/spec/guardian.spec meta.version, guardian/registry-components.js const V, which pins itself to lib/version.js under §5.4 and would otherwise have drifted the moment the bump landed. New spec block built_2026_09_23_account_authority_and_job_evidence records: accounts moved to Clear Glass, with the deliberate asymmetry of its two failure modes (an explicit account that cannot be resolved FAILS the job, because dispatching into the wrong signed-in session is worse than not dispatching; Clear Glass merely being unreachable falls back to NCP, because an authority that is down must not take the engine with it); job stage evidence and the new guardian.job.progress event, now also listed in events.emits; and the .job file being required at creation, including an explicit already_existed_do_not_rebuild note that loadPersistedJobs + _redispatchRecoveredJobs (2026-09-11) already implement the restart gate, so a future pass does not rebuild them. The open item (Clear Glass job intake) is recorded in the spec as open_not_done with the reason, not silently dropped. Atlas gains two sections in its own idiom: "Jobs are files" and "A quiet job says where it stopped". NEW tests/modules/test-system-record-discipline.js 9/9 makes the standing rule enforceable rather than an intention: one version string per system across version.js / spec / registry for EVERY system that declares both, guardian's spec asserted by name to record the authority, the .job change and the new event, and every atlas that exists asserted to be real content rather than a stub (a stub atlas is worse than none -- it reads as documented when it is not). It deliberately does NOT attempt to prove "the spec describes the code": no test can, and pretending otherwise would be a worse lie than the gap it claims to close -- stated in the file header. Mutation-checked: drifting lib/version.js -> 3 fail; removing the authority from the spec -> 1 fail. Regression job-correlation 102, job-persistence 15, repo-agent 35, repo-inject 79. NOT CHANGED, checked rather than assumed: guardian/interaction-contract.json stays at 53 routes and registry-components gains no entries -- this work added an event and an internal file-write rule, not HTTP surface, so regenerating either would have been churn.]   // [0.39.225: handoff item 5, part 1: the .job file is the source of truth at creation. MAPPED FIRST, and the handoff's premise was half wrong -- worth recording: it said "nothing reads the file back" and treated the .job as a side copy. Boot recovery and REDELIVERY both already exist (guardian/lib/jobs.js loadPersistedJobs seeds the in-memory Map from disk at createJobStore; guardian/server.js _redispatchRecoveredJobs, built 2026-09-11, walks the recovered jobs, requeues 'dispatched' -> 'pending' because the tab that held it is provably gone in a fresh process, then dispatches each pending job once and leaves complete/error as history). So the stated gate "kill guardian mid-job -> restart -> re-queued once, never duplicated" was already implemented; this pass PROVES it rather than rebuilding it. What was genuinely missing: _persistJob was non-fatal in every case, so a job whose file never landed was still accepted -- the file could not be the source of truth if its absence changed nothing. Now the write is required at CREATION (recoverability is the promise createJob makes; a job that cannot survive a restart is refused loudly with a message naming the directory and the reason, §1.2) and stays non-fatal on later status updates (the job already exists and may be mid-flight; killing real in-flight work because a status write failed would lose more than it protects, and it degrades restart fidelity for that one job, loudly). Also: JOBS_DIR was hardcoded, so the directory that is now the source of truth could not be named -- GUARDIAN_JOBS_DIR overrides it, default unchanged, and JOBS_DIR is exported. NEW tests/modules/test-guardian-job-persistence.js 15/15 against the real createJobStore with a real restart (a second store constructed over the same directory, the way a fresh process does): creation writes the file with the real prompt, a status update is written through, a fresh process recovers the job exactly as left without duplicating the file, an in-flight job is requeued exactly once and that requeue is a real state change on disk, a completed job is recovered as history and never requeued with its answer intact, and a job whose file cannot be written is refused with a reason naming recoverability. Mutation-checked: creation write made non-fatal again -> 2 fail; status writes not persisted -> 5 fail. Regression job-correlation 102, repo-agent 35. NOT DONE, and deliberately not guessed: Clear Glass watching the jobs directory and adding each .job to its intake. The mesh dom-transport queue is already idempotent by jobId and the NCP path already delivers these same jobs, so wiring a second delivery route without deciding which one owns delivery would create exactly the duplicate-dispatch class this item exists to prevent -- that is a design decision for James (watch vs push, and which transport owns the job once both can see it).]   // [0.39.224: job stage evidence: a job that goes quiet now says WHERE it stopped. Ported the work in flight on the 0.39.218 tree when James moved to 0.39.223; everything else from 215-218 was already present in 223 and was NOT re-applied (checked per symbol: _baseEl, _stampAgent, agentTabs, NEXUS_AGENT_ID, repo-agents-section, chat_url, job-complete log). THE EVIDENCE THIS ANSWERS: James's 0.39.218 run showed job created -> dispatched -> ACKED -> silence, and his screenshot showed ChatGPT had actually answered on screen. The prompt was sent and the RESULT never came back -- but nothing could say which step failed: submit() returned nothing (a send that never happened and one that worked were indistinguishable) and the watch reported nothing until a generic no-reply timeout. Now submit() in all five userscripts returns ok/how (button|enter-key) or ok:false with the reason nothing could send; a prompt typed but never sent fails IMMEDIATELY with that reason instead of waiting out NO_REPLY_MS, and the watch is not started for it; the watch emits GUARDIAN_PROGRESS stage reply-started with a character count the first time it sees text that is not the stale baseline, placed BEFORE the baseline guard so 'the guard is skipping it' is distinguishable from 'the page produced nothing'. ncp-handler routes GUARDIAN_PROGRESS to guardian.job.progress; server logs one line per stage with the chat url. The next run's log separates four previously identical silences: never sent, sent but nothing rendered, rendered but never settled, and returned. test-guardian-job-correlation 60 -> 102 against the real startWatch per provider. Mutation-checked, and the first attempt was CAUGHT AS TOO WEAK: a source-text assertion for reply-started passed while the branch was disabled with a false guard, so it was replaced by a behavioural assertion on emitted messages, which does fail (101/1); fail-fast removal fails (91/1). Regression repo-agent 35, repo-inject 79, repo-context 16, repo-toolbar-mode 23. Also carried, not mine: James's in-flight settings work already modified in the uploaded tree (core.js hashchange re-render, settings.css tweak), committed with attribution rather than reverted.]   // [0.39.223: Clear Glass is the account authority; login portals; OS-sealed vaults; Settings
  // rebuilt. James: "login portals like apppassword login to each provider, with a plus to add multiple, which hook
  // into the cookie vault, and create a accountid per account... agent mesh and suite, macros, erosmanceros, all of it"
  // then "yes clearglass" (decides D3 of the mesh-first phasemap). (1) AUTHORITY: guardian/lib/agent-registry.js kept
  // its own account list, never exposed; Clear Glass's options store held the real accounts -> an account made in
  // Clear Glass never reached dispatch, the mesh opened mesh-<provider>-default, a session nobody signed into. Now:
  // options store gains accountDefaults + resolveAccountForDispatch (never auto-creates) + per-account session records;
  // Clear Glass serves GET :7702/accounts/resolve; guardian/lib/cg-account-authority.js asks it; the ladder awaits it
  // and on Clear Glass down falls back to NCP with reason account_authority_unreachable (never a silent default).
  // (2) PORTALS: clear-glass/src/accounts/login-portal.js opens a provider's sign-in in EXACTLY persist:mesh-<provider>-
  // <accountId> (the partition agent-mesh spawn() uses), sandboxed; capture writes the session into CookieVault under
  // {agentId:provider, accountId} (the key spawn() restores) and links the signed-in identity through the existing
  // collision-checked linkProviderAccount, refusing BEFORE any write. No provider issues app passwords, so "saved
  // sign-in" = PasswordVault credential pre-filled into the page, never submitted. (3) VAULT KEYS: both vaults' keys
  // moved off hardcoded strings onto Electron safeStorage (clear-glass/src/security/vault-key.js) with legacy-key read
  // fallback, so no existing record becomes unreadable; unavailable keyring is reported, not hidden. (4) REAL BUGS
  // FIXED on the way: CookieVault's file-store branch (the only one on a real install -- better-sqlite3 is not a
